From 2d94ec7aa7a65943ea68b78b51f3c4b6429cb873 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 08:07:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(android): browser-process DisplayXR runtime fd connector
 (#1056)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

M2 step 1 of the Android port (docs/android-port.md). On Android the OpenXR
session that owns the weave must live in Chromium's GPU process, but connecting
to the runtime service is a Java affair — Context, <queries> package visibility,
cross-apk class loading, bindService — all of which belong to the browser
process. So the browser connects once and ships the socket down; the GPU process
adopts it and never binds anything (runtime spec
docs/specs/runtime/android-ipc-fd-adoption.md).

DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java mirrors the reference client's
RuntimeFdConnector.kt: reflective load of org.freedesktop.monado.ipc.Client out
of the installed runtime apk, Client(long) with a non-zero sentinel, blocking-
Connect(applicationContext, runtimePkg), read the `fd` field and hand out dups.
Two lifetime rules are load-bearing and commented as such — the Client instance
is retained for the browser-process lifetime (it owns the satellite-slot claim
and the slot death link), and every handoff is a dup so the original survives the
adopter.

warmUp() runs the 283-372 ms connect off the critical path;
takeFdForChildProcess() only blocks if a child launches before it lands.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
